<main id="main-content">
<div class="blog-wrap">
<article>
  <div class="blog-bc"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/blog/">Blog</a> &rsaquo; Good Cash on Cash Return</div>

  <div class="blog-header">
    <h1>What Is a Good Cash on Cash Return for Rental Properties in <%= currentYear %>?</h1>
    <div class="blog-meta">
      <span>By <strong>Sarah Mitchell</strong>, Real Estate Analyst</span>
      <span class="blog-badge">✓ Audited for <%= currentYear %> Guidelines</span>
      <span class="blog-cat">💵 Real Estate ROI</span>
    </div>
    <p class="blog-intro">
      For real estate investors who utilize mortgage financing, <strong>Cash-on-Cash (CoC) Return</strong> is the single most vital metric of success. While Cap Rate measures unlevered asset efficiency, Cash-on-Cash Return tells you the exact cash yield earned on every actual dollar you pulled from your bank account to close the deal.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="snippet-box">
    <div class="sq">Cash-on-Cash Return Benchmark Guide (<%= currentYear %>)</div>
    <p class="sa">
      &bull; <strong>Target Good Benchmark:</strong> 8% – 12%<br>
      &bull; <strong>Formula:</strong> (Annual Pre-Tax Cash Flow &divide; Total Cash Invested) &times; 100<br>
      &bull; <strong>Below 6%:</strong> Low cash flow (coastal markets / appreciation play)<br>
      &bull; <strong>Above 12%:</strong> High-yield cash flow (Midwest / secondary markets / value-add BRRRR)
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section">
    <h2>Worked Step-by-Step Example</h2>
    <p>
      Let's examine an investor purchasing a duplex for <strong>$350,000</strong> using standard conventional investment debt:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Purchase Price:</strong> $350,000</li>
      <li><strong>Equity Down Payment (25%):</strong> $87,500</li>
      <li><strong>Closing Costs &amp; Escrows:</strong> $8,500</li>
      <li><strong>Total Cash Invested (Out-of-Pocket):</strong> <code>$87,500 + $8,500 = $96,000</code></li>
      <li><strong>Loan Amount:</strong> $262,500 (30-yr fixed at 7.0%, Annual P&amp;I = $20,957)</li>
      <li><strong>Net Operating Income (NOI):</strong> $31,500 (After all operating expenses, vacancy, taxes, insurance)</li>
      <li><strong>Annual Pre-Tax Cash Flow:</strong> <code>$31,500 (NOI) - $20,957 (Debt Service) = $10,543</code></li>
    </ul>
    <div style="background:#f1f5f9;padding:12px;border-left:4px solid #0369a1;font-family:monospace;font-weight:700;margin:1rem 0;">
      Cash-on-Cash Return = ($10,543 &divide; $96,000) &times; 100 = 10.98%
    </div>
    <p>
      In this example, the investor earns a <strong>10.98% cash yield</strong> in Year 1 on their $96,000 out-of-pocket investment, placing this property firmly in the "Good to Strong" category.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section">
    <h2>The 2026 Cash-on-Cash Return Scale</h2>
    <table class="blog-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>CoC Return Range</th>
          <th>Rating</th>
          <th>Typical Market Type</th>
          <th>Investor Rationale</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Below 5%</strong></td>
          <td>Poor Cash Yield</td>
          <td>Los Angeles, San Francisco, NYC, Boston</td>
          <td>Negative or near-zero leverage. Only justified if expecting 6%+ annual capital appreciation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>5% – 7.9%</strong></td>
          <td>Acceptable / Moderate</td>
          <td>Denver, Austin, Miami, Phoenix, Seattle</td>
          <td>Solid markets with growing populations; cash flow covers debt with modest dividend.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>8% – 11.9%</strong></td>
          <td>Good / Target Standard</td>
          <td>Atlanta, Tampa, Dallas-Fort Worth, Charlotte, Columbus</td>
          <td>The benchmark goal for most buy-and-hold residential real estate investors.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>12% – 16%+</strong></td>
          <td>Exceptional / High Yield</td>
          <td>Cleveland, Memphis, Detroit, Kansas City, BRRRR projects</td>
          <td>High cash generation; typically older properties requiring active hands-on management.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section">
    <h2>Positive vs. Negative Leverage Explained</h2>
    <p>
      The relationship between your mortgage interest rate and the property's cap rate dictates whether debt helps or hurts your return:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Positive Leverage (Cap Rate &gt; Interest Rate):</strong> When you borrow money at 6.0% on a property with an 8.0% cap rate, financing magnifies your returns, boosting cash-on-cash return above 10%.</li>
      <li><strong>Negative Leverage (Cap Rate &lt; Interest Rate):</strong> If you borrow at 7.5% on a property with a 5.5% cap rate, the cost of debt eats up cash flow, pulling your cash-on-cash return down to 2%–3%.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-cta">
    <h3>Run Your Property Numbers Automatically</h3>
    <p>Calculate NOI, debt service, pre-tax cash flow, and exact cash-on-cash returns with our free tool.</p>
    <a href="/real-estate/cash-on-cash-return-calculator">Open Cash-on-Cash Calculator &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="blog-faq-list">
      <div class="blog-faq-item open">
        <div class="blog-faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">What is considered a good cash-on-cash return in 2026?</div>
        <div class="blog-faq-a">In 2026, an 8% to 12% cash-on-cash return is widely considered good to excellent for residential real estate investments. A return between 6% and 8% is acceptable in high-growth coastal markets where investors anticipate strong long-term property appreciation. A return above 12% indicates exceptional cash flow, commonly achieved in secondary Midwest markets or through value-add renovations.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-faq-item">
        <div class="blog-faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">What is the formula for cash-on-cash return?</div>
        <div class="blog-faq-a">Formula: Cash-on-Cash Return = (Annual Pre-Tax Cash Flow / Total Cash Invested) × 100. Annual Pre-Tax Cash Flow equals Net Operating Income (NOI) minus annual mortgage principal and interest payments. Total Cash Invested equals down payment + closing costs + immediate renovation expenses.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-faq-item">
        <div class="blog-faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">Is cash-on-cash return better than cap rate?</div>
        <div class="blog-faq-a">Neither is inherently better; they serve different purposes. Cap rate measures the property's intrinsic earning power assuming an all-cash purchase, making it ideal for comparing different properties. Cash-on-cash return evaluates your actual financial return based on the specific debt financing, interest rate, and down payment you use.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-faq-item">
        <div class="blog-faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">How does mortgage interest rate affect cash-on-cash return?</div>
        <div class="blog-faq-a">Because cash-on-cash return is calculated after mortgage debt service, higher interest rates directly reduce your annual cash flow and lower your return. When mortgage rates exceed a property's cap rate, "negative leverage" occurs, meaning financing the property actually reduces your cash-on-cash yield compared to buying all cash.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>

